Fibromyalgia Post-Traumatic Onset
Complex Fatigue Entities
Pain amplification following physical trauma

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-016
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of acute, generalized pain crisis and extreme muscle rigidity following minor physical exertion.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported sudden onset of unendurable total-body pain and near-complete immobility. Immediate physical assessment was mandated to rule out an occult delayed fracture or acute rhabdomyolysis, and to provide potent central analgesia to halt severe distress prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My entire body feels like it was crushed in a vice, and I jump at the slightest sound.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a severe acute exacerbation of generalized pain originating from a motor vehicle accident 2 years ago. Over the last 12 hours, the patient developed profound hyperalgesia, severe systemic stiffness rendering them unable to bend their spine, and extreme psychological hypervigilance. The timeline shows a linear escalation from mild focal ache to severe generalized burning pain.
Functional Status: Bedbound, unable to transition from supine to standing independently.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high risk of medication error due to panic and desperation for pain relief.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/90 mmHg | HR: 105 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 20 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 76 Kg
General: Visibly tense, highly reactive to environmental stimuli, shifting continuously in bed.
Physical Exam: Marked hyperesthesia to light touch across all four quadrants of the body. Severe paraspinal muscle spasm without palpable bony step-offs. Range of motion is globally restricted by pain apprehension. Neurological exam reveals intact motor strength and normal reflexes. Presentation confirms pain amplification following physical trauma rather than new structural deficit.
Environmental Observations: Blinds drawn; patient highly sensitive to ambient light and noise.
Medication Reconciliation: Consumed daily allowance of over-the-counter NSAIDs within a 4-hour window with zero clinical effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Fibromyalgia Post-Traumatic Onset - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute spinal fracture - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of focal bony point tenderness and intact peripheral neurology.

Rhabdomyolysis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear urine output without myoglobinuria and absence of recent extreme physical crush injury.

Post-traumatic stress disorder panic attack - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Prominent, distinct physical allodynia and muscle spasm dominating the clinical picture, despite comorbid hypervigilance.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's presentation of sudden, excruciating, unremitting body pain and functional collapse required immediate after-hours, face-to-face evaluation. It was clinically imperative to rule out delayed structural failure from prior trauma and acute systemic muscle breakdown. Having confirmed a severe post-traumatic central sensitization flare, urgent administration of central nervous system down-regulators was necessary to break the pain-panic cycle, avoiding an inevitable and inappropriate emergency department transfer for a non-surgical pain crisis.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral Diazepam 5mg once only to break acute paraspinal spasm and hypervigilance. Prescribed Celecoxib 200mg daily. Targeted intervention strategy includes COX-2 inhibitors combined with low dose THC/Cannabinoids, and Magnesium.
Positive Behavioral Change: By systematically practicing 5 minutes of progressive muscle relaxation before attempting to move from the bed, you will actively override your body's trauma response, directly decreasing muscle guarding and rewarding yourself with a smoother, less painful physical transition.
Home-Based Services Optional: Referral to trauma-informed physical therapy.
DME/Safety Optional: Weighted blanket recommended for sensory grounding.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days to optimize chronic neuro-modulators.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department immediately if you lose control of your bowel/bladder, develop sudden focal weakness in any limb, or if your urine turns the color of dark tea.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis reflects a functional neuro-inflammatory state; no new structural damage has been identified tonight.
Clinical Course: Centralized flares triggered by physical stress generally peak within 24-48 hours and slowly recede as the central nervous system recalibrates.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 if your legs suddenly give way completely or if you wet yourself without realizing.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your nervous system is mistakenly replaying the alarm signals from your original injury; the muscle relaxant acts as a chemical circuit breaker to stop this false alarm.
Actionable Steps: Keep your room quiet and dimly lit tonight; reducing external sensory input prevents your overactive nervous system from finding new triggers, reinforcing a faster return to your baseline pain level.

